-- title:
--   Non-negativity of the angle between two lines
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space. For vectors $v, h \in E$ let $\angle_{\mathrm{line}}(v, h)$ denote the *angle between the lines* spanned by $v$ and by $h$ — the quantity written `dangle v h`, which is the angle between $v$ and $h$ read modulo replacing either vector by its negative, so that it is unchanged when $v$ or $h$ is reflected through the origin. It is the quantity for which membership $h \in V(v, \vartheta)$ in a double cone with axis $v$ and aperture $\vartheta$ says exactly $\angle_{\mathrm{line}}(v, h) < \vartheta$. Then, for all $v, h \in E$,
--   $$0 \le \angle_{\mathrm{line}}(v, h).$$
--
--   Non-negativity is one of the two elementary facts making $\angle_{\mathrm{line}}$ behave like a distance on the space of lines; it is used, together with the triangle inequality for this quantity, when a covering of the unit sphere by small angular caps is converted into an inclusion between double cones.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement carries no hypothesis whatsoever: $v$ and $h$ are arbitrary vectors of an arbitrary real inner product space, in particular they need not be unit vectors and either may be zero. In the degenerate cases the value is whatever the underlying angle convention returns, and the lemma asserts only that this value is never negative.
